  "query_preview": "..Visible satellite imagery shows cumulus cloud \ndevelopment this afternoon across the higher terrain of southeast \nArizona. The area is under easterly flow aloft with mid/upper level \nhigh near the Four Corners. Still plenty of moisture for much of \nsoutheast Arizona with PWAT around 1 to 1.5 inches, highest values \nwest. Cochise County PWAT is a bit lower in the 0.7 to 0.9 inch \nrange and that area will struggle most with convection this \nafternoon into evening. CAMs are showing two primary areas of storms \ndeveloping this afternoon, the first being south to southwest of \nTucson close to the Int'l border and another area moving off the \nWhite Mountains with storm movement today towards the west. Given \nthe ample surface heating, elevated DCAPE values will support gusty \nwinds in excess of 50 mph with the strongest storms. In fact, the \nHREF has an area of 10% 40-km neighborhood probability of 50 kts \nfrom Tucson westward with a smaller area of 30 percent over central \nand western Pima County. These strong winds will also result in \nareas of blowing dust due to strong outflows. In addition to the \ngusty winds, a few storms may cluster west of Tucson resulting in \nisolated flash flooding potential into the evening hours. \n\nMonday is shaping up to be a bit more active than today as easterly \nflow aloft continues with PWAT values ticking up slightly compared \nto today and modest mid/upper level support for storms. This will \nresult in another round of showers and thunderstorms for most of \nsoutheast Arizona, with the highest chances from Tucson westward. \nWPC has highlighted the area in a SLGT risk of excessive rainfall \nand that looks good. HREF has a widespread area of greater than 50% \n40-km neighborhood probability of 3-hr QPF of 1 inch or greater. \nWhile there is still some uncertainty on storm mode for Monday, and \nwhether storms stay more discrete or become more clustered resulting \nin heavier rain potential especially into the evening, felt it was \nprudent to highlight the potential for heavy rain by hoisting a \nFlash Flood Watch from Tucson area westward from 2 PM to 10 PM. In \naddition to the flash flood threat, severe winds will also be a \nthreat with considerable DCAPE and even around 20-30 kts of 0-6km \nbulk shear brining the potential for small hail as well. Given the \nwind potential, added in areas of blowing dust north and west of \nTucson into the forecast.\n\nThe active pattern will persist Tuesday and Wednesday with Wednesday \nstill looking like a more active day as a disturbance in northern \nSonora may help aid storm development then. Thereafter, as southwest \nflow moves in starting Thursday, that will shift storm chances from \nabout the Tucson area eastward Thursday, then generally near the NM \nborder Friday through next weekend with most of SE Arizona remaining \ndry those days. \n\nTemperatures will lower to near normal levels Monday and Tuesday, \nthen to about 3 to 6 degrees below normal Wednesday and Thursday, \nwith warming thereafter to above normal values for next weekend.",
